                "Unit economics ask whether each customer is worth more than they cost to win and serve. Customer acquisition cost (CAC) is sales and marketing spend divided by new customers in a period. Lifetime value (LTV) is often estimated as average revenue per account x gross margin / churn rate. A common rule of thumb is LTV:CAC of 3 or more; CAC payback (months of gross-margin-adjusted revenue to recover CAC) under about 12 months is considered healthy for many SaaS businesses, longer for enterprise. David Skok's SaaS metrics guide is the standard explainer. Treat these as estimates: LTV is very sensitive to the churn assumption.",
                "Gross margin is revenue minus the cost of delivering it (hosting, third-party services, support, payment fees), as a percentage of revenue. Traditional software often runs 70-80%+; AI-heavy products can be much lower because model inference costs scale with usage, which makes pricing and model choice strategic. Contribution margin goes further by including variable sales costs.",
